portable-pty 0.8.1: close the child's inherited descriptors without /dev/fd

close_random_fds() (run in the forked child before exec) closes every fd above
stdio that /dev/fd lists.  anonymOS has no /dev/fd and, more importantly, no
close-on-exec at all (fcntl F_SETFD/O_CLOEXEC are accepted and ignored), so
nothing was closed: the Wayland socket, eventfds and EGL descriptors leaked into
the shell, and so did the write end of std::process's exec-status pipe -- whose
read end the parent blocks on until EOF, i.e. until the shell EXITS.  ratty
would hang in spawn_command() before ever opening its window.

After the /dev/fd pass, close everything above stdio with close_range(2), or
(ENOSYS there too) one by one up to RLIMIT_NOFILE.  On Linux close_range
succeeds at once and nothing changes.

Applied by scripts/build-ratty.sh to the checksum-verified portable-pty-0.8.1
.crate unpacked in deps/ratty/anonymos-crates/.
---
diff --git a/src/unix.rs b/src/unix.rs
index 53389eb..ab67336 100644
--- a/src/unix.rs
+++ b/src/unix.rs
@@ -112,9 +112,9 @@ impl Read for PtyFd {
 /// 3 or higher, effectively closing all descriptors except for the stdio
 /// streams.
 ///
-/// The implementation of this function relies on `/dev/fd` being available
-/// to provide the list of open fds.  Any errors in enumerating or closing
-/// the fds are silently ignored.
+/// The listed fds come from `/dev/fd`; anything not listed there is then
+/// closed with close_range(2), or one by one up to RLIMIT_NOFILE.  Any errors
+/// in enumerating or closing the fds are silently ignored.
 pub fn close_random_fds() {
     // FreeBSD, macOS and presumably other BSDish systems have /dev/fd as
     // a directory listing the current fd numbers for the process.
@@ -140,6 +140,29 @@ pub fn close_random_fds() {
             }
         }
     }
+
+    // Without a listable /dev/fd, or on a kernel that has no close-on-exec (anonymOS), whatever
+    // was not listed above would leak into the child -- including std::process's exec-status
+    // pipe, whose parent end then never sees EOF, so spawn() blocks until the shell exits.
+    // Close everything above stdio: close_range(2) where the kernel has it, else one by one
+    // up to the descriptor limit.
+    #[cfg(any(target_os = "linux", target_os = "android"))]
+    if unsafe { libc::syscall(libc::SYS_close_range, 3 as libc::c_uint, libc::c_uint::MAX, 0) } == 0 {
+        return;
+    }
+    let mut limit: libc::rlimit = unsafe { mem::zeroed() };
+    let max_fd = if unsafe { libc::getrlimit(libc::RLIMIT_NOFILE, &mut limit) } == 0
+        && limit.rlim_cur != libc::RLIM_INFINITY
+    {
+        limit.rlim_cur.min(65536) as libc::c_int
+    } else {
+        1024
+    };
+    for fd in 3..max_fd {
+        unsafe {
+            libc::close(fd);
+        }
+    }
 }
 
 impl PtyFd {
